//? Title: MADE: Two Degree-Masked Joint Densities
//? Subtitle: Strict causal paths, reverse prefixes and a normalized probability mixture
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Degree masks turn a dense network into a normalized autoregressive density:
every output can depend only on earlier inputs in a chosen order. This native
C++ experiment fits two independent binary8x8 MNIST models, raster and reverse,
with two16-unit sigmoid layers each. Worked degree and mixture examples precede
the real data. The complete program exposes all192 Adam writes and both raster
samplers, including order-specific observed prefixes. The fixed-final losses are
.55804 and.55103 bits per binary pixel; their equal-probability joint mixture
scores.55218. All remain worse than the fit-only Bernoulli baseline's.38445.
Exact source parity and causal checks establish numerical fidelity, not
state-of-the-art density estimation or realistic digit generation.
)
\toc

> Use degree constraints to exclude own and future inputs

[Germain, Gregor, Murray and Larochelle, MADE: Masked Autoencoder for
Distribution Estimation (2015)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1502.03509) constrains dense
network paths so outputs define conditional probabilities. Here the word
masked refers to **structural weight masks**, not random input corruption or a
denoising reconstruction objective. The target is a normalized Bernoulli joint
density over64 binary pixels.

Give each input/output a degree d. Input-to-hidden and hidden-to-hidden masks
permit incoming degree<=outgoing degree. The last mask is strict: hidden degree
<output degree. Along every surviving path,

$$ d_i\le h^{(1)}_u\le h^{(2)}_v<d_j,\qquad\text{so }d_i<d_j. $$
\alttext(Every permitted path has nondecreasing hidden degrees and a strict final inequality, so an output depends only on lower-degree inputs.)

Thus logit j excludes its own pixel and every later pixel in that order. Two
sigmoid hidden layers of width16 end in64 Bernoulli logits. There are no raw
input skips, labels, normalization, dropout, pooling or batch-coupled statistics.
The first prediction has only its learned bias. Some past inputs can also be
unreachable with the chosen small hidden-degree inventory; strict causality
does not promise every past dependency.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/md_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
 std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
 dm::graphics::Diagram d(54,1600);dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle s;
 s.width=10;s.height=5;s.font_size=34;
 d.node("x",{-21,0},"64 binary inputs\nDegree d",s)
 .node("h1",{-7,0},"16 sigmoid units\nh1 >= input degree",s)
 .node("h2",{7,0},"16 sigmoid units\nh2 >= h1",s)
 .node("out",{21,0},"64 Bernoulli logits\nOutput degree > h2",s)
 .edge("x","h1").edge("h1","h2").edge("h2","out")
 .save_svg("build/md_method.svg");
}
